  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 338 AM EDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis and GOES water vapor imagery show a pair \nof weak disturbances embedded in southwest flow aloft, one over \nnorthern Ontario and another over Lower Michigan. Radar reveals \nspotty shower activity departing the far eastern UP in association \nwith these features, with broken mid level cloud cover and some \npatchy fog in place across the rest of Upper Michigan. Temperatures \nwill spike well above normal today as continued warm advection sends \n850 mb temps to 9 to 11 C over the area this afternoon. The NBM is \nadvertising high temperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s this \nafternoon. Feel this might be slightly overdone given the degree of \ncloud cover in place. Regardless, still expect temperatures to run \n15-20 degrees ahead of late October norms this afternoon as high s \nreach at least the low to mid 60s.\n\nThe next system approaches the area tonight as a surface low over \nthe central Plains tracks quickly northeastward to western Lake \nSuperior by later this evening, bringing a quick hitting round of \nrain to the UP from west to east tonight into early Thursday \nmorning. Rainfall remain in line with previous forecast cycles, \ngiving a roughly 50-75% chance for a wetting rain of a quarter inch \nacross the western third of the UP and closer to a 10-25% chance for \nthe central and eastern UP through Thursday morning. Could see a few \nrumbles of thunder accompany this activity given a few hundred J/kg \nMUCAPE. SPC even has a marginal risk for severe weather into the far \nwestern. Modest instability would seemingly be a significant \nlimiting factor to this potential, but can't completely rule out a \nfew stronger storms given bulk shear values of 40-50 kt by late this \nevening.\n\nA cold front crosses the UP Thursday morning, with precipitation \nquickly winding down in the east as surface high pressure builds in \nfrom the northern Plains. This will drop highs back down into the \n50s in most locations on Thursday afternoon, with breezy west-\nnorthwest winds also expected, especially in the Keweenaw and along \nthe Lake Superior shoreline where winds will likely gust to 30+ mph. \nCooler and drier weather continues into Friday as surface high \npressure moves directly overhead. There is still a high degree of \nmodel spread beyond Friday as high pressure departs to the Atlantic \nthis weekend. Guidance hints at a stronger system moving out of the \nPlains and approaching the Great Lakes during the early part of next \nweek, but with a lot of variation with respect, to track, timing, \nand intensity.",
